  "final_text": "I built the dashboard and created the alert rule, and the rule is enabled. Its first run finished with status `ok`, so the query runs without errors. I haven't seen it fire or opened the dashboard to look at the charts.\n\n**1. What the data holds:** each document in `kibana_sample_data_logs` is one web request. The fields include:\n- **Time:** `timestamp`, which the data view uses as its time field.\n- **Visitor:** `clientip`, `agent`, `machine.os` and location fields (`geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`).\n- **Request:** `request`, `url`, `extension`, `referer`, `host`.\n- **Result:** `response` (the HTTP status code, stored as text) and `bytes`.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-eeb164b7 Web logs overview\"** (http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-eeb164b7-web-logs-overview-39e8ed1447b3) has 7 panels:\n- Total requests\n- Unique visitors (distinct client IPs)\n- Total bytes served\n- Requests over time (line chart)\n- Breakdown of response codes (pie chart)\n- Top 10 requested URLs, with request count and bytes (table)\n- Top 10 source countries (bar chart)\n\n**3. Alert rule \"EFF-eeb164b7 5xx spike\"** (id `db92a867-89af-42cd-ae5c-843e1413d98a`):\n- It checks `kibana_sample_data_logs` every minute.\n- It fires when more than 10 requests with a response code from 500 to 599 arrive within the last 5 minutes, measured on `timestamp`.\n- Because the response code is stored as text, the rule matches it as a text range from \"500\" up to \"600\", which picks out exactly the 5xx codes.\n- No notification action is attached, so when it fires the alert only appears in Kibana and nobody is notified. If you want a Slack, email or other notification, tell me which and I'll add it.",
